<!--
  ============================================================================================
  ANALYST DETERMINATIONS FOR THE SUPPLY-CHAIN GATE

  Every entry in this file is a finding this module has examined and can demonstrate does not
  apply to the bytes it resolves. Nothing here lowers the gate: the threshold stays at CVSS
  7.0, the test graph stays in scope, and the scan stays bound to `verify`. What an entry does
  is record a determination, with its evidence, in the one place a reviewer will look for it.

  THREE RULES GOVERN WHAT MAY BE WRITTEN HERE, and they are the reason this file is short.

  1. A fix outranks a determination. If a patched version exists, the version moves and no
     entry is written. An entry is only ever the answer when there is nothing to move to.
  2. A determination needs a false-match argument that can be checked, not an opinion that the
     risk is tolerable. "The vulnerable code is not in this artifact, and here is the command
     that shows it" qualifies. "This is probably not exploitable here" does not. Where the
     vulnerable classes are physically present, this module narrows the scanned scope and
     discloses that instead of writing an entry - see the Gate 8 section of README.md, which
     records that decision and why it went the other way.
  3. Every entry is scoped to named artifacts and one named identifier. No blanket `<cve>`
     without an artifact scope, no wildcard `<cpe>`, no `<gav>` regex broad enough to absorb a
     future finding that nobody has looked at. Where an entry names more than one artifact, the
     evidence has to cover every one of them individually.

  EACH ENTRY EXPIRES BY ITSELF, WHICH IS WHY NO ENTRY CARRIES A REVIEW DATE. The plugin runs
  with `failBuildOnUnusedSuppressionRule` set true. The moment a rule stops matching - because
  a patched version was adopted, because the coordinate left the graph, or because the upstream
  record was corrected - the build fails on the unused rule and the entry has to be removed. A
  calendar date would be a second, weaker trigger that fires whether or not anything changed;
  the unused-rule check fires exactly when the determination has become obsolete. That property
  is the whole reason the flag was already enabled before this file existed.

  `BuildAndCiContractTest` asserts the shape of this file and of the configuration that reads
  it, so a later edit cannot quietly widen a rule, drop the scoping, or disarm the flag that
  makes the entries self-expiring.
  ============================================================================================
-->
<suppressions xmlns="https://jeremylong.github.io/DependencyCheck/dependency-suppression.1.3.xsd">

    <!--
      CVE-2026-66299 (CVSS 7.5 HIGH) - Uncontrolled resource consumption in Apache Tomcat's
      WebSocket chat EXAMPLE, matched against the embedded servlet container by version alone.

      WHY THERE IS NOTHING TO UPGRADE TO. The advisory names 10.1.58 as the fixed release on the
      10.1 line (`versionEndExcluding` 10.1.58 in the NVD record) and 11.0.25 on the 11 line.
      Neither is published. A direct HEAD against Maven Central returns 404 for 10.1.58, 10.1.59,
      10.1.60 and 11.0.25, and the newest 10.1.x that resolves is 10.1.57 - the version this
      module already pins upward, deliberately, in `pom.xml`. The advisory has been published
      ahead of the artifact, so rule 1 above has no fix to prefer. Re-check with:

          curl -sI -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code}\n' \
            https://repo1.maven.org/maven2/org/apache/tomcat/embed/tomcat-embed-core/10.1.58/tomcat-embed-core-10.1.58.jar

      WHY THE FINDING DOES NOT REACH THIS MODULE. The vulnerable component is the examples web
      application that ships in the full Tomcat distribution under `webapps/examples`, and the
      advisory says so itself: "Users who have followed the security guidance to remove the
      examples web application are not affected by this issue." A Spring Boot application embeds
      the container as a library and has no `webapps` directory to deploy an examples application
      from. That is not an inference - the three embedded jars on this graph carry 2,036 entries
      between them and not one of them is a `webapps/`, `examples/` or chat-example entry:

          for a in tomcat-embed-core tomcat-embed-websocket tomcat-embed-el; do
            unzip -l ~/.m2/repository/org/apache/tomcat/embed/$a/10.1.57/$a-10.1.57.jar \
              | awk '{print $4}' | grep -cE 'webapps/|examples/|websocket/chat|ChatAnnotation'
          done      # -> 0, 0, 0   (entry totals 1681, 191, 164)

      WHY THE MATCH HAPPENED ANYWAY. The scanner matched on the product-level platform record
      `cpe:2.3:a:apache:tomcat:10.1.57`, which addresses the Tomcat product at that version rather
      than any file inside it. The report carries no evidence tying the match to the examples
      application, because there is none in the artifact to find.

      SCOPE OF THIS RULE, AND WHY IT NAMES THREE ARTIFACTS RATHER THAN THE ONE THAT WAS FLAGGED.
      The product CPE is assigned per artifact, and which of the embedded jars receives it is not
      stable between scans. Two runs six minutes apart, over the same graph and the same pinned
      version, attributed this finding differently: the first reported it against
      `tomcat-embed-core` only, and the second - after `core` was suppressed - reported it against
      `tomcat-embed-websocket`, which had carried the identical pair of Tomcat CPEs all along.
      `tomcat-embed-el` currently carries no Tomcat CPE at all, only its package URL, so it cannot
      be matched today. Reproduce that reading from the report itself:

          python3 -c "import json;d=json.load(open('target/dependency-check-report.json'));\
          [print(x['fileName'],[i['id'] for i in x.get('packages',[])]) \
           for x in d['dependencies'] if 'tomcat' in x['fileName']]"

      Scoping the rule to whichever jar happened to be named in the last scan would leave the gate
      failing the next time the assignment moved, for a determination that had already been made.
      So the rule names all three embedded jars, and it may do that under rule 3 above only
      because the evidence covers all three individually: the archive listing was taken for each
      one separately and none of the three contains a single examples entry. It is one library, one
      version, one advisory and one measurement - not a family wildcard.

      The version is left open in the regex so the rule keeps matching while 10.1.57 remains the
      newest published release. It stops matching, and therefore fails the build on the unused
      rule, the moment a patched line is adopted - which is the intended way for this entry to
      end.

      Recorded in ../docs/decision-log.md DL-159 with the full measurement.
    -->
    <suppress>
        <notes><![CDATA[
            Apache Tomcat examples web application finding matched against the embedded container
            by version alone. No patched artifact is published (10.1.58 and 11.0.25 are both 404 on
            Maven Central), and the vulnerable examples application is not present in any of the
            three embedded jars this module resolves - 0 of 2,036 archive entries across them are
            webapps, examples or chat-example entries. All three are named because the product CPE
            migrated between them across consecutive scans. See ../docs/decision-log.md DL-159.
        ]]></notes>
        <packageUrl regex="true">^pkg:maven/org\.apache\.tomcat\.embed/tomcat-embed-(core|websocket|el)@.*$</packageUrl>
        <cve>CVE-2026-66299</cve>
    </suppress>

</suppressions>
